  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Severe thunderstorms are near-certain for Tuesday.\nWidespread severe weather and scattered flash flooding are \nexpected.\n\nSevere weather is once again on the docket. Tuesday (Day 2) has\nbeen consistently highlighted by SPC for many days as a\npotentially active and dangerous weather day. The threat for\ndamaging wind is very certain, and could occur anywhere over \nthe CWA Tuesday afternoon and evening. The threat for hail is\nhigher than they were this past Saturday, and the threat for\ntornadoes is about the same.\n\nA potent upper trough will dig into the Great Lakes tonight and\nearly Tuesday. Warm air will surge northward in the morning\nhours. Some showers and weaker storms will roll through southern\nand eastern PA late tonight, perhaps helping to raise dewpoints.\nIt looks like a pre- frontal trough will charge in from the NW \nand cross the CWA in the afternoon hours. The real cold front\nwill arrive later in the evening/early night. It may arrive too\nlate (some stabilization could already happen) and have little \nleft to work with by the time it gets into the CWA to make much \nof a severe threat. \n\nThere is also a flooding threat, but only if there will be a \nlocation or two that gets multiple rounds of heavy rain. There \nshould be a good movement to the storm cells, limiting the \nthreat somewhat. However, moisture is expected to be very high, \nPWATs 1.5-2\". \n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Thicker wildfire smoke should stay mainly over \nwestern PA, with levels barely noticeable in the east. \n\nAn air quality alert/action day remains in effect for most of\nsouthwestern PA where levels are expected to be unhealthy for\nsensitive groups. HRRR smoke plume progs keep the thicker smoke\nover western PA with much less over the east. The light wind\nthis morning will turn from easterly to southerly through the\nday which will help to keep the near-sfc smoke from spreading \nto the east.",
